# external-scan configuration file
#
# Copy this file to external-scan.conf in the same directory. That copy holds
# your real targets and is ignored by git.
#
# LEGAL: only list IP addresses you have WRITTEN permission to scan. Ask the
# owner to whitelist the scanning machine in CrowdSec, their IDS, fail2ban or
# similar, or the scan gets blocked halfway.
#
# One setting per line, fields separated by spaces. "#" starts a comment.
# A line the script does not understand stops the scan with its line number.
#
# ===========================================================================
# EXAMPLE
# ===========================================================================
#
# A firewall that forwards web and mail to a server behind it, then a router
# with no name to test. Remove the "# " at the start of each line to use it.
# Global settings have no indentation, the lines under a target do.
#
# pdf_report on
# operator "Jane Doe, Example IT"
#
# target 203.0.113.10 firewall.example.com
#   authorization "purchase order 2026-042, signed by J. Smith"
#   web_scan on
#   https www.example.com   443
#   https cloud.example.com 443
#   smtp  mail.example.com  25
#   smtp  mail.example.com  587
#   tls   mail.example.com  465
#   imap  mail.example.com  143
#   tls   mail.example.com  993
#   accept sensitive/9090 until 2027-06-30 "firewall GUI, VPN planned"
#
# target 198.51.100.20 office router
#   authorization "purchase order 2026-042, signed by J. Smith"
#
# The router block has no name to test. Its open ports are still found and
# tested on the IP alone, see "What a target line alone does" below.
#
# ===========================================================================
# GLOBAL SETTINGS (all optional, no indentation)
# ===========================================================================
#
# cert_warn_days <days>
#   Flag a certificate that expires in fewer days. Default: 30
cert_warn_days 30
#
# keep_months <months>
#   Delete older results after each scan that ends without ERROR. Default: 12
keep_months 12
#
# pdf_report on | off
#   Also write REPORT_<name>_<date>_<time>.pdf per target, in English, for the
#   owner: who scanned, when, what and how, the findings, and the SHA-256 of
#   every raw result. A failed PDF is noted in SUMMARY.txt and scan.log and
#   never changes the status. Default: off
#
# operator <text>
#   Who runs the scan, printed in the PDF.
#
# web_scan on | off
#   Run nuclei, a web vulnerability scanner, on every "https" line. It finds
#   outdated applications behind a reverse proxy, exposed admin pages, files
#   like .git or .env. Critical and high templates only, without dos,
#   intrusive and fuzz, at most 20 requests per second. Templates are
#   downloaded again at each scan. This is a vulnerability scan: turn it on
#   only when the written permission covers it. A target can override it.
#   Default: off
#
# udp_ports <port,...> | none
#   REPLACES the list of UDP ports scanned on every target. "none" turns UDP
#   off. Without this line, the default list at the end of this file is used.
#   All TCP ports are always scanned. UDP only uses a list because a full UDP
#   scan takes about 18 hours. UDP needs the raw socket right, so it only
#   runs from systemd.
#
# udp_ports_extra <port,...>
#   ADDS ports to the UDP list, default or not, without copying it. Several
#   lines add up.
#   Example: udp_ports_extra 9987,27015
#
# sensitive_ports <port,...> | none
#   REPLACES the list of TCP ports that are risky to expose: remote access,
#   admin pages, databases, file sharing. Each one open gives a finding
#   ("sensitive/<port>") every month, until it is closed or accepted. "none"
#   turns the check off. Without this line, the default list at the end of
#   this file is used.
#
# ===========================================================================
# TARGETS (one block per public IP address)
# ===========================================================================
#
# target <ip> <label>
#   <ip>     ONE IPv4 address. IPv6, ranges and host names are refused. The
#            scan never connects to any other address for this block.
#   <label>  Free text for the report and the result folder. Its first word
#            names the PDF.
#
# What a target line alone does, on the IP without any name:
#   - nmap finds every open TCP port, the listed UDP ports, the software
#     behind each and its known CVEs,
#   - testssl.sh checks every open port that nmap sees as TLS, and every
#     SMTP, IMAP or POP3 port through STARTTLS,
#   - ssh-audit checks every SSH port.
# Without a name, a reverse proxy answers with its default certificate, so
# the real certificates of the sites behind it are not checked.
#
# Lines under a target, all optional and indented:
#
#   <mode> <fqdn> <port>
#   Test this name on this port of the IP above. The name is sent in the TLS
#   handshake (SNI), so a reverse proxy shows the certificate of that site.
#   The connection always goes to the IP above, whatever the DNS says. The
#   report says when the name resolves to another address. A port that nmap
#   does not see open is skipped with a note.
#   <mode> is one of:
#     https                TLS, the HTTP checks of testssl.sh, and nuclei when
#                          web_scan is on. For web sites.
#     tls                  TLS from the first byte, any protocol: 465, 993,
#                          995, or anything else. smtps, imaps and pop3s are
#                          accepted too and do exactly the same as tls.
#     smtp | imap | pop3   Plain text first, then STARTTLS: 25 and 587, 143,
#                          110.
#
#   web_scan on | off
#   Overrides the global web_scan for this target.
#
#   authorization <text>
#   The written permission for this address, printed in the PDF.
#
#   accept <key> until <YYYY-MM-DD> <reason>
#   Accept a known risk, so it stops turning the status to ATTENTION. The
#   report lists it under "Accepted risks" with the reason. It comes back
#   after the date. An accept line that matches nothing is noted in the
#   report. You rarely write the key by hand: the report ends with ready to
#   copy accept lines. "*" in the key matches anything.
#   Keys:
#     tls/<name or ip>/<port>/<testssl id>   TLS finding
#     cert/<name or ip>/<port>               certificate expires soon
#     ssh/<port>/<kex|key|enc|mac>/<alg>     weak SSH algorithm
#     ssh/<port>/cve/<CVE id>                SSH CVE
#     sshpw/<port>                           SSH accepts passwords
#     sensitive/<port>                       sensitive port open
#     dns/<name>                             name does not resolve to the IP
#     web/<name>/<port>/<template id>        nuclei finding
#     failed/tls/<name or ip>/<port>         testssl.sh failed
#     failed/ssh/<port>                      ssh-audit failed
#     failed/sshpw/<port>                    SSH login check failed
#     failed/web/<name>/<port>               nuclei failed
#     failed/udp                             UDP scan failed
#
# ===========================================================================
# DEFAULT PORT LISTS (for reference)
# ===========================================================================
#
# udp_ports:
#   7,17,19,53,67,69,88,111,123,137,138,161,162,389,427,443,464,500,514,520,
#   623,853,1194,1434,1701,1719,1812,1813,1900,2049,2427,3283,3389,3478,3702,
#   4500,4569,5060,5160,5349,5351,5353,5683,10001,11211,37810,47808,51820,
#   55820
#   NethServer 8: cluster WireGuard (55820), Samba AD (88, 464, 137, 138),
#   DHCP (67). Telephony: SIP (5060, 5160), IAX2 (4569), H.323 (1719), MGCP
#   (2427), STUN/TURN (3478, 5349). VPN: IPsec (500, 4500), OpenVPN (1194),
#   L2TP (1701), WireGuard (51820). Others: DNS, DNS over QUIC, TFTP, rpcbind,
#   NTP, SNMP and traps, CLDAP, QUIC, IPMI, MS-SQL browser, RADIUS, syslog,
#   RIP, NFS, SSDP, RDP, mDNS, NAT-PMP, Ubiquiti discovery, memcached, and
#   the amplification classics: echo, qotd, chargen, SLP, Apple Remote
#   Desktop, WS-Discovery, CoAP, DVR (37810), BACnet.
#
# sensitive_ports:
#   21,23,135,139,445,902,1433,2082,2083,2086,2087,2222,2375,2376,2379,2380,
#   3306,3389,4848,5000,5001,5432,5601,5900,5901,5985,5986,6379,6443,7001,
#   8006,8007,8161,8291,8443,8728,8729,8834,8880,9000,9090,9200,9443,9990,
#   10000,10250,11211,15672,19999,20000,27017
#   Admin panels: cPanel and WHM (2082, 2083, 2086, 2087), DirectAdmin (2222),
#   Plesk (8443, 8880), Proxmox VE and PBS (8006, 8007), Cockpit (9090),
#   Portainer (9000, 9443), Webmin and Usermin (10000, 20000), Synology DSM
#   (5000, 5001), Netdata (19999), Kibana (5601), Nessus (8834), RabbitMQ
#   (15672), ActiveMQ (8161), Java servers (4848, 7001, 9990). Remote
#   management: ESXi (902), WinRM (5985, 5986), MikroTik (8291, 8728, 8729),
#   Kubernetes and etcd (2379, 2380, 6443, 10250). File sharing and
#   databases: FTP (21), Telnet (23), SMB (135, 139, 445), MS-SQL (1433),
#   MySQL (3306), PostgreSQL (5432), Redis (6379), Elasticsearch (9200),
#   MongoDB (27017), memcached (11211), RDP (3389), VNC (5900, 5901), Docker
#   (2375, 2376).
